    "created_at": "2022-03-03T07:33:32Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-03-03T07:33:32Z",
    "body": "I'm fairly certain that an unauthenticated user cannot disable logging on sudo attempts: else it would be trivial to make a brute-force attack on a sudo password undetectable by just asking that the attempts not be logged.\r\n\r\nIf you have root access to the system, you should be able [disable sudo logging for your user or for a specific subset of commands](https://stackoverflow.com/a/14278264) by editing sudoers. The most specific approach I can think of would be to turn off logging for variants of `true`:\r\n\r\n```\r\nCmnd_Alias TRUE = /bin/true, /usr/bin/true\r\nDefaults!TRUE !syslog\r\n```\r\n\r\nHowever, note that this will still leave you blind to someone trying to guess your sudo password by repeatedly running attemtping to run `true` with `sudo`. Whether this is an acceptable risk or not is up to you.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
